# R23 the Silent Church as a shelter. By hand, polished by Gemini, unvoiced.

~ arrive
Narrator: The chanting from the ridge dies cleanly at the threshold, leaving only the smell of damp wool and cold lime. Pews have been dragged together for the sick, children sleep heaped in coats along the altar steps, and the marble knight stands two men tall in the nave, listening toward the fire.
set wv_shelter_seen
-> END

~ marble
Narrator: His voice settles behind your eyes like cold stone, never touching the air.
man_in_marble [neutral]: Bring the frail beneath this roof and the hill's singing will not reach them. My feet are rooted to this floor. I can stand, and I can listen. It is the only work I was carved for.
-> marble_menu

~ marble_menu
* [if flag.sleep_count >= 1] Give him the count of those inside -> marble_count
* Ask what the chanting does to a mind -> marble_song
* Ask if the church walls will hold -> marble_hold
* Leave him to his watch -> END

~ marble_count
man_in_marble [neutral]: I know the weight of every soul across that threshold. Those under my roof, I will keep. Those still beyond the doors are past my reach.
-> marble_menu

~ marble_song
man_in_marble [sad]: It seeks out the cracked timber in a mortal mind and sets it humming until it splinters. Under this roof, a flaw is only a flaw.
-> marble_menu

~ marble_hold
man_in_marble [neutral]: Against the song, this silence is a fortress. Against a torch, it is only dry timber and dressed stone. Keep the fire on the hill.
-> marble_menu

~ weston
weston_murdoch [smile]: Even a Brightsinger hymn drops dead a foot from my lips in here. I hum them anyway, down low, so the little ones hear a friendly throat. Go win the night. Or if you can't, make sure they see you swinging.
-> END
